{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ונקה האיש מעון", :translit "ve-nikah ha-ish me-avon", :gloss "and the man shall be cleared of iniquity"} {:he "ונקה", :translit "ve-nikah", :gloss "and he shall be cleared"} {:he "ג' תגין על ה\"א", :translit "shloshah tagin al ha-heh", :gloss "three crowns on the heh"} {:he "שנקה מחמשה עונות", :translit "she-nikah me-chamishah avonot", :gloss "that he is cleared of five iniquities"} {:he "עון אשת איש", :translit "avon eshet ish", :gloss "the iniquity of a married woman"} {:he "עון פנויה", :translit "avon penuyah", :gloss "the iniquity of an unmarried woman"} {:he "עון שלא בא עליה", :translit "avon she-lo ba aleha", :gloss "the iniquity of [one] who did not have relations with her"} {:he "עון בניו", :translit "avon banav", :gloss "the iniquity of his sons"} {:he "עון בנותיו", :translit "avon benotav", :gloss "the iniquity of his daughters"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Three tagin on the heh of ונקה signal clearance from five categories of iniquity, matching the numerical value of the letter heh (5)."}], :frame {:text "The sotah ritual resolves uncertainty of suspected infidelity; Numbers 5:31 concludes that the husband bears no guilt for subjecting his wife to the procedure. Ba'al HaTurim reads the calligraphic ornaments on the word ונקה as encoding five distinct scenarios of clearance, expanding beyond the plain sense of the verse.", :sources ["Numbers 5:31"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את עונה", :translit "et avonah", :gloss "her iniquity"} {:he "ונשא את עונה", :translit "ve-nasa et avonah", :gloss "and he/it shall bear her iniquity"} {:he "בנדרים", :translit "be-nedarim", :gloss "in Nedarim"} {:he "שלא ידאג הבעל לומר גרמתי לה מיתה", :translit "she-lo yeda'ag ha-ba'al leimor garmati lah mitah", :gloss "that the husband should not worry to say I caused her death"} {:he "אלא היא תשא את עונה", :translit "elah hi tisa et avonah", :gloss "rather she shall bear her iniquity"} {:he "שהיה יכול להפר ולא הפר לה", :transli